    "body": "Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang delivers remarks as President Donald Trump looks on during an “Investing in America” event on April 30, 2025, at the White House. Credit: Official White House Photo by Joyce N. Boghosian.\nPosted By: Northern Miner Staff November 23, 2026\nUranium stocks rallied Monday after Nvidia (Nasdaq: NVDA) brought back co-founder Jensen Huang as CEO. Cameco (TSX: CCO; NYSE: CCJ) gained 7.4% in New York, recovering some of the ground uranium miners lost when Huang stepped down this month.\nCameco traded at $62.30 (C$89.24) around 9:42 a.m. Toronto time, while Energy Fuels (TSX: EFR; NYSE-A: UUUU) climbed 11% to $5.51. The Sprott Uranium Miners ETF (NYSE Arca: URNM) rose 6.3% to $35.74 as Nvidia jumped 26% to $158.11.\n“Uranium stocks used to trade on uranium. Now they trade with AI,” David Talbot, a mining analyst and head of equity research at Red Cloud Securities, wrote Monday.\nTech, shares link\nTechnology companies’ deals to buy nuclear power for their data centres helped drive uranium shares higher last year, Talbot said. The same link punished miners when artificial intelligence stocks fell. From Jan. 28 through Nov. 19, the Sprott fund lost 57% even as uranium market consultant UxC’s long-term price for uranium oxide (U 3 O 8 ) rose 10% to $97 per pound.\nNvidia’s board voted unanimously Sunday to reinstate Huang , whose departure the chipmaker announced Nov. 12. After hearing from senior U.S. officials, directors concluded his return posed no national security risk. Independent directors are still investigating reports that a foreign intelligence operation targeted him, Nvidia said Monday .\nThe sell-off around Huang’s departure fit a pattern Talbot traced back to July 2025. On the 58 trading days when a fund tracking AI stocks fell more than 1.5%, Sprott’s miners fund fell 88% of the time, he found.\nNuclear demand rising\nDemand for reactor fuel has kept growing. Nearly 90 gigawatts of nuclear capacity was under construction worldwide by midyear, the most in more than 35 years, the International Energy Agency said in its Nov. 19 outlook . Utilities still need to contract for about 768 million lb. of U 3 O 8 through 2035, Talbot said, citing UxC.\n“Nothing in this morning’s news changes reactor demand or uranium supply, so it does not change the fundamental case for the uranium stocks,” Talbot wrote after Nvidia announced Huang’s return.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Uranium\nREGION: United States\nCOMPANY: Cameco Energy Fuels Nvidia Sprott Uranium Miners ETF\nRepublish this article",
